WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.280
The dial is the BMP side of the choice between skin and neural.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.680
In frog caps and in the whole embryo, FGF is needed too.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.800
Where else does the rule stop?

4
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:17.163
First limit: it says neural, not where.

5
00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:20.623
Noggin caps made forebrain and cement gland markers.

6
00:00:21.717 --> 00:00:24.537
They made no hindbrain or spinal cord markers.

7
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:27.997
Neural tissue made by Noggin in caps is anterior.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:34.097
This fits an older idea from Nieuwkoop: two steps, activation and transformation.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.097
Activation makes ectoderm neural and anterior.

10
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:42.597
Transformation shifts part of it toward hindbrain and spinal cord.

11
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.130
In molecular terms, the BMP antagonists look like activators.

12
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.220
Wnt signals, FGFs and retinoic acid are leading candidates for transformation.

13
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:58.963
The spinal cord may follow a different order.

14
00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:03.823
Much of it may come from progenitors made posterior before they become neural.

15
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:06.417
Second limit: the chick.

16
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:11.837
Read blindly, the rule says blocking BMP anywhere should make neural tissue.

17
00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:18.897
In 1998, Claudio Stern's laboratory made Chordin in chick epiblast outside the

18
00:01:18.897 --> 00:01:19.897
future neural plate.

19
00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:21.857
Do those cells turn neural?

20
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.130
No. They made no early neural markers.

21
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.090
Blocking BMP there was not sufficient.

22
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.840
Yet Chordin did stabilize early neural markers in cells that had already received

23
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.040
neural-inducing signals.

24
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.493
In 2000, Andrea Streit and colleagues looked earlier.

25
00:01:49.453 --> 00:01:54.753
FGF signaling before gastrulation is needed to begin neural induction in chick.

26
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:59.757
In 2001, Sara Wilson and colleagues added Wnt.

27
00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:03.897
Its state decides how epiblast cells respond to FGF.

28
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:07.507
So in chick, two more inputs join the dial.

29
00:02:08.407 --> 00:02:13.667
One is early FGF. The other is a Wnt state that lets cells answer it.

30
00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.147
Third limit: the mouse.

31
00:02:16.147 --> 00:02:20.927
Embryos lacking both chordin and noggin have severe forebrain defects.

32
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.090
Yet they still form other neural tissue.

33
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.810
So the two are needed for a normal forebrain, but not for all neural tissue.

34
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:35.203
A fair summary: BMP inhibition is needed for the frog neural plate and for mouse

35
00:02:35.203 --> 00:02:36.203
forebrain.

36
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.727
In chick epiblast, it is not sufficient on its own.

37
00:02:40.647 --> 00:02:42.847
None of this shows the frog results were wrong.

38
00:02:43.817 --> 00:02:45.277
So, back to the one idea.

39
00:02:46.157 --> 00:02:48.497
In early frog caps, BMP says skin.

40
00:02:49.357 --> 00:02:54.317
Turn its signal down far enough, and competent cells become neural, as long as other

41
00:02:54.317 --> 00:02:56.517
inputs such as FGF are intact.

42
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.440
The takeaway: the dial is the BMP branch of the choice between skin and neural.

43
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.420
FGF and competence are part of that choice too, in caps and embryos.

44
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.500
Where the tissue lies along the body, and how chick and mouse differ, are further

45
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:12.960
questions.
